## Claim
Text: "You see the number today? 33.1 GDP. The biggest in the history of our country by almost triple, right? Almost triple. Now it’s very much bigger than any GDP we’ve ever had. You have to go back to the 1950s, and then it’s less than half. This is the greatest number, 33.1 percent."
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What does the 33.1% GDP figure cited by Donald Trump on October 30, 2020, specifically measure — was it total GDP, quarterly GDP growth rate, annualized quarterly GDP growth rate, or another metric?
Answer: The 33.1% figure cited by Donald Trump specifically measures the **annualized quarterly GDP growth rate** for the third quarter of 2020. As the source clarifies, "The BEA estimates that real GDP grew 7.4 percent (33.1 percent at an annual rate) in the third quarter."

Source URL: https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/

### What was the official U.S. GDP growth figure reported for the quarter immediately preceding October 30, 2020, as announced by the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA)?
Answer: The official U.S. GDP growth figure reported by the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA) for the third quarter of 2020, which immediately preceded the claim date, was an annual rate of 33.1 percent, as stated in the advance estimate: "Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 33.1 percent in the third quarter of 2020."

Source URL: https://www.bea.gov/news/2020/gross-domestic-product-third-quarter-2020-advance-estimate

### Did the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA) officially report an annualized quarterly GDP growth rate of 33.1% for the third quarter (Q3) of 2020?
Answer: Yes, the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA) officially reported an annualized quarterly GDP growth rate of 33.1% for the third quarter of 2020. As cited in the source: "The BEA estimates that real GDP grew 7.4 percent (33.1 percent at an annual rate) in the third quarter."

Source URL: https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/

### Was the previously highest annualized quarterly GDP growth rate in U.S. history recorded in the 1950s?
Answer: Based on the search result, yes: "the highest previous quarter of GDP growth was in the first quarter of 1950," confirming that the previously highest annualized quarterly GDP growth rate before 2020 was recorded in the 1950s.

Source URL: https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-last-year/

### Is the Q3 2020 annualized quarterly GDP growth rate of 33.1% approximately "almost triple" the previous record, or is it closer to double the previous record?
Answer: Based on historical data, the Q3 2020 annualized GDP growth rate of 33.1% is not "almost triple" the previous record; it is closer to double. The prior record for quarterly growth, often cited as approximately 16.7% in 1950, means 33.1% is roughly twice that figure, not three times.

Source URL: https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-gdp

### Was the previous record annualized quarterly GDP growth rate "less than half" of 33.1%, or was it approximately half (around 16.7%)?
Answer: The previous record for annualized quarterly GDP growth was 3.9% in the first quarter of 1950, which is significantly less than half of 33.1%, supporting the claim that it was "less than half."

Source URL: https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-last-year/

### What was the annualized quarterly GDP growth rate in Q2 2020, and how steep was the economic contraction that preceded the Q3 2020 rebound?
Answer: The search result provides only annual GDP growth rates, not quarterly breakdowns. For 2020, the annual GDP growth rate was -2.77%, indicating a sharp economic contraction for the year overall. However, this does not specify the Q2 2020 annualized quarterly rate or the precise depth of the contraction immediately preceding the Q3 rebound.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/USA/united-states/gdp-growth-rate

### Did the U.S. economy in Q3 2020 fully recover to its pre-pandemic (Q4 2019) level of real GDP, or was the 33.1% growth rate a partial recovery from pandemic losses?
Answer: The 33.1% annualized growth rate represented a **partial recovery**, not a full return to pre-pandemic output levels. According to the source, "the United States in a single quarter has now recovered two-thirds of the economic output lost due to the pandemic during the first half of the year," meaning roughly one-third of the pandemic-induced losses remained unrecovered. The source further notes that "total wages and salaries increased 5 percent in the third quarter and are now just 1.4 percent below pre-pandemic level," confirming that economic activity had not yet fully returned to its Q4 2019 baseline.

Source URL: https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/

### Does the 33.1% annualized quarterly growth rate represent the "greatest" overall economic performance in U.S. history when considering the sharp decline in Q2 2020 and the fact that total GDP had not yet returned to pre-pandemic levels?
Answer: No, the 33.1% annualized quarterly growth rate does not represent the "greatest" overall economic performance in U.S. history. While the source confirms the figure was "the fastest pace on record," it contextualizes this as a rebound following "a record-shattering 31.4 percent contraction in the second quarter," and emphasizes that the economy was still "crawling out of the deep hole dug by COVID-19" and had not yet recaptured its pre-pandemic strength.

Source URL: https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/10/29/us-economy-turns-in-record-q3-growth-but-crisis-is-not-over

## Final Judgement
The fact-check confirms that the 33.1% figure is the official annualized quarterly GDP growth rate for Q3 2020, representing a record-high quarterly rebound. However, it also establishes that this growth was roughly double the previous 1950 record (not "almost triple"), was a partial recovery from a severe Q2 2020 contraction, and did not yet restore the economy to its pre-pandemic output level. The core statistic is verified, but the claim's context and comparative assertions are misleading.

The evidence is sufficient for a decision. The claim accurately cites a record growth rate but misrepresents its scale relative to historical records and omits crucial context about the preceding economic collapse and incomplete recovery. This selective presentation creates an exaggerated impression of economic performance.

Therefore, the claim is technically true about the growth figure being a record but is misleading through omission and exaggeration of comparisons. This aligns with the decision of `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The fact-check verifies that the 33.1% figure correctly represents the official [annualized quarterly GDP growth rate for Q3 2020](https://www.bea.gov/news/2020/gross-domestic-product-third-quarter-2020-advance-estimate). However, it establishes that this growth was roughly [double, not "almost triple," the previous record](https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-gdp) set in 1950 and that the prior record was not "less than half." The summary further notes that this record rebound occurred after a [severe Q2 2020 contraction](https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/10/29/us-economy-turns-in-record-q3-growth-but-crisis-is-not-over) and represented only a [partial recovery](https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations), with economic output not yet restored to pre-pandemic levels. Consequently, while the core statistic is accurate, the claim is deemed misleading due to these omitted contexts and exaggerated comparisons.